</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Initialization<span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span>
<span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tanbr</span> begins by initializing the neural network, where each element of the parameter vector <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜽</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is randomly generated from an appropriate Gaussian distribution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. Furthermore, the partition tree is initialized with a root node that spans the entire decision space, <span id="S4.SS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, <math id="S4.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}=[0,1]^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}=[0,1]^{K}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Decision Making<span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span>
At each time slot <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S4.SS3.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tanbr</span> gets the task feature vector <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝍</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and extracts the set of active leaf nodes <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒩</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> from the partition tree <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> (lines <a href="#alg1.l3" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-C Design of Tanbr ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>-<a href="#alg1.l4" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-C Design of Tanbr ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>).
For each active node, a feasible merging weight is generated that satisfies both its cover region and constraint (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In III-C Problem Formulation ‣ III System Model ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>a), forming the candidate set (line <a href="#alg1.l6" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-C Design of Tanbr ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). Then, for each merging weight <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{h,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{h,i}</annotation></semantics></math>, line <a href="#alg1.l7" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-C Design of Tanbr ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> calculates its UCB <math id="S4.SS3.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{h,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">U</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{h,i}</annotation></semantics></math> by,</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle U_{h,i}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">U</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle U_{h,i}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{t}^{\top}f(\boldsymbol{x}_{h,i},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{t-1})+\nu_{1}\rho^{h}+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝍</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜽</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ν</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">ρ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi></msup></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\boldsymbol{\psi}_{t}^{\top}f(\boldsymbol{x}_{h,i},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{t-1})+\nu_{1}\rho^{h}+</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gamma_{t-1}\sqrt{\boldsymbol{g}(\boldsymbol{x}_{h,i},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{t-1})^{\top}\boldsymbol{Z}_{t-1}^{-1}\boldsymbol{g}(\boldsymbol{x}_{h,i},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{t-1})/w}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">γ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒈</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜽</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒁</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒈</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜽</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gamma_{t-1}\sqrt{\boldsymbol{g}(\boldsymbol{x}_{h,i},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{t-1})^{\top}\boldsymbol{Z}_{t-1}^{-1}\boldsymbol{g}(\boldsymbol{x}_{h,i},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{t-1})/w},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒁</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is an auxiliary variable;
<math id="S4.SS3.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">γ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive scaling factor and is defined as
<math id="S4.SS3.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{t}=\sqrt{1+C_{1}w^{-1/6}\sqrt{\log w}L^{4}t^{7/6}\lambda^{-7/6}}\cdot(\upsilon\sqrt{\log(\det\boldsymbol{Z}_{t}/\det\lambda\boldsymbol{I})+C_{2}w^{-1/6}\sqrt{\log w}L^{4}t^{5/3}\lambda^{-1/6}-2\log\delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">γ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">6</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></mrow></msqrt><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">4</mn></msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">7</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">6</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">7</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">6</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">υ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒁</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">det</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝑰</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">6</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi></mrow></msqrt><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">4</mn></msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">5</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn></mrow></msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">6</mn></mrow></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{t}=\sqrt{1+C_{1}w^{-1/6}\sqrt{\log w}L^{4}t^{7/6}\lambda^{-7/6}}\cdot(\upsilon\sqrt{\log(\det\boldsymbol{Z}_{t}/\det\lambda\boldsymbol{I})+C_{2}w^{-1/6}\sqrt{\log w}L^{4}t^{5/3}\lambda^{-1/6}-2\log\delta}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<math id="S4.SS3.p8.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="+\sqrt{\lambda}S)+(\lambda+C_{3}tL)[(1-\eta dw\lambda)^{1/2}\sqrt{t/\lambda}+w^{-1/6}\sqrt{\log w}\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mi>λ</mi></msqrt><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mi>t</mi><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mi>d</mi><mi>w</mi><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em">⋅</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+\sqrt{\lambda}S)+(\lambda+C_{3}tL)[(1-\eta dw\lambda)^{1/2}\sqrt{t/\lambda}+w^{-1/6}\sqrt{\log w}\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>
<math id="S4.SS3.p8.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="L^{7/2}t^{5/3}\lambda^{-5/3}(1+\sqrt{t/\lambda})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>λ</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{7/2}t^{5/3}\lambda^{-5/3}(1+\sqrt{t/\lambda})]</annotation></semantics></math></span>,
where <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the regularization parameter, <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> is the norm parameter, and <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> represents the step size for updating the parameters of the neural network.
The first term estimates the expected reward, the second reflects the granularity of the node’s region in the tree, and the third term quantifies the uncertainty in the reward prediction.
Line <a href="#alg1.l8" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-C Design of Tanbr ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> selects merging weight <math id="S4.SS3.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{h_{t},i_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒙</mi><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{h_{t},i_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> with the highest upper bound.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Parameter Updating<span id="S4.SS3.p9.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span> The selected merging weight <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{h_{t},i_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{h_{t},i_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> is used to merge experts, and the merged model is deployed for fine-tuning/inference.
Afterward, the corresponding reward <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}(\boldsymbol{x}_{h_{t},i_{t}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}(\boldsymbol{x}_{h_{t},i_{t}})</annotation></semantics></math> is observed (line <a href="#alg1.l10" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-C Design of Tanbr ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).
<span id="S4.SS3.p9.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tanbr</span> then updates the selection count <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{h,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{h,i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all active leaf nodes in <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒩</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (line <a href="#alg1.l12" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-C Design of Tanbr ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). If the selected node has been explored sufficiently, <span id="S4.SS3.p9.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, the number of times the node has been selected <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{h_{t},i_{t}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">P</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{h_{t},i_{t}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds the threshold <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{h_{t}}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{h_{t}}(t)</annotation></semantics></math>, it is expanded (lines <a href="#alg1.l13" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-C Design of Tanbr ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>-<a href="#alg1.l15" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-C Design of Tanbr ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>). As described in Sec. <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Tree-Structured Measurable Space Partitioning ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>, the expansion is performed by randomly selecting a dimension of the region for binary partitioning, which results in two new leaf nodes being added to <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>.
At the end of <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, line <a href="#alg1.l17" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-C Design of Tanbr ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> updates the auxiliary variable <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{Z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒁</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{Z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and line <a href="#alg1.l18" title="In Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-C Design of Tanbr ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> updates the neural network parameters <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜽</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by minimizing the loss function <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(\boldsymbol{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜽</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(\boldsymbol{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math> using stochastic gradient descent (SGD) with a step size <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math>. The loss function we used is:
<math id="S4.SS3.p9.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L(\boldsymbol{\theta})={\textstyle\sum_{s=1}^{t}}\frac{1}{2}\left(f(\boldsymbol{x}_{h_{s},i_{s}},\boldsymbol{\theta})-f^{*}(\boldsymbol{x}_{h_{s},i_{s}})\right)^{2}+\frac{\lambda}{2}\|\boldsymbol{\theta}-\boldsymbol{\theta}_{0}\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜽</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msubsup><mrow><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜽</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜽</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜽</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L(\boldsymbol{\theta})={\textstyle\sum_{s=1}^{t}}\frac{1}{2}\left(f(\boldsymbol{x}_{h_{s},i_{s}},\boldsymbol{\theta})-f^{*}(\boldsymbol{x}_{h_{s},i_{s}})\right)^{2}+\frac{\lambda}{2}\|\boldsymbol{\theta}-\boldsymbol{\theta}_{0}\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.SS3.p9.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>, where <math id="S4.SS3.p9.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the regularization parameter.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Theoretical Analysis</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To facilitate a more concise and structured theoretical analysis, we augment the neural network with an additional linear output layer parameterized by <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝍</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let the complete set of parameters be denoted by <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\xi}=[\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\top},\boldsymbol{\psi}_{t}^{\top}]^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝃</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝜽</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝍</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\xi}=[\boldsymbol{\theta}^{\top},\boldsymbol{\psi}_{t}^{\top}]^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math>. Accordingly, the estimated and real total reward is expressed as <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{\xi})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝃</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{\xi})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{1}^{*}(\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{1}^{*}(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math>. All detailed proofs are provided in the Appendix.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Candidate Arms Upper Bound<span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span>
We first write some assumptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="ThmAssumption1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="ThmAssumption1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 1</span></span><span id="ThmAssumption1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="ThmAssumption1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="ThmAssumption1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="ThmAssumption1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i) </span><span id="ThmAssumption1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Dissimilarity)</span><span id="ThmAssumption1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The space <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is equipped with a dissimilarity function <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell:\mathcal{X}\times\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime})\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≥</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime})\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{x})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{x})=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
ii) Define the diameter of a subset <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒜</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> as
<math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="diam(\mathcal{A})=\sup_{\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{y}\in\mathcal{A}}\ell(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒜</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0.1389em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒜</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐲</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">diam(\mathcal{A})=\sup_{\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{y}\in\mathcal{A}}\ell(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{y})</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-open ball of radius <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">&gt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and center <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> as
<math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}(\boldsymbol{x},\epsilon)=\{\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}\in\mathcal{X}:\ell(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime})\leq\epsilon\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ϵ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}(\boldsymbol{x},\epsilon)=\{\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}\in\mathcal{X}:\ell(\boldsymbol{x},\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime})\leq\epsilon\}</annotation></semantics></math>.
iii) </span><span id="ThmAssumption1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Local Smoothness)</span><span id="ThmAssumption1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">

We assume that there exist constants <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1},\nu_{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ν</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ν</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">&gt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1},\nu_{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\rho&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ρ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\rho&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all nodes <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(h,i)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(h,i)</annotation></semantics></math>:
a) <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="diam(\mathcal{H}_{h,i})\leq\nu_{1}\rho^{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ν</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">ρ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">diam(\mathcal{H}_{h,i})\leq\nu_{1}\rho^{h}</annotation></semantics></math>;
b) There exists <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}_{h,i}^{o}\in\mathcal{H}_{h,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}_{h,i}^{o}\in\mathcal{H}_{h,i}</annotation></semantics></math> such that:
<math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{h,i}=\mathcal{B}(\boldsymbol{x}_{h,i}^{o},\nu_{2}\rho^{h})\subseteq\mathcal{H}_{h,i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℬ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">o</mi></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ν</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">ρ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊆</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{h,i}=\mathcal{B}(\boldsymbol{x}_{h,i}^{o},\nu_{2}\rho^{h})\subseteq\mathcal{H}_{h,i}</annotation></semantics></math>;
iii) <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}_{h,i}\cap\mathcal{B}_{h,j}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">∩</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℬ</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}_{h,i}\cap\mathcal{B}_{h,j}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">≠</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>,
iv) For all <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="ThmAssumption1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{*}-f(x)\leq\ell(\boldsymbol{x}^{*},\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{*}-f(x)\leq\ell(\boldsymbol{x}^{*},\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The dissimilarity assumption measures the difference between any two points in space. The local smoothness assumption ensures that the space is smoothly partitioned: regions shrink as depth increases, are non-overlapping, and the function behaves smoothly within each region.</p>
</div>
<div id="ThmLemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="ThmLemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="ThmLemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="ThmLemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="ThmLemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="ThmLemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the threshold <math id="ThmLemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{h}(t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{h}(t)</annotation></semantics></math> defined in Eq. (<a href="#S4.E2" title="In IV-B Tree-Structured Measurable Space Partitioning ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), the number of candidate merging weights at <math id="ThmLemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall t</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded by:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{t}\leq N=2^{1/(1-\rho)}T\nu_{1}^{2}/\left(C^{2}\log(T/\delta)\right)=\mathcal{O}(T)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">ρ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">ν</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{t}\leq N=2^{1/(1-\rho)}T\nu_{1}^{2}/\left(C^{2}\log(T/\delta)\right)=\mathcal{O}(T).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Regret Upper Bound<span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span>
The regret of an algorithm is defined as the difference between the total expected reward achieved by the optimal offline solution and that obtained by
the algorithm. Let <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the merging weight with the highest reward, <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">e.g.</span>, <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}^{*}=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{t}\arg\max_{\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathcal{X}}f^{*}(\boldsymbol{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">x</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝝍</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">max</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}^{*}=\boldsymbol{\psi}_{t}\arg\max_{\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathcal{X}}f^{*}(\boldsymbol{x})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The regret of <span id="S4.SS4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tanbr</span> is defined as follows:
<math id="S4.SS4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R={\textstyle\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}}}R_{t},\hskip 9.24994ptR_{t}=\mathbb{E}\left[f_{1}^{*}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t}^{*})-f_{1}^{*}(\boldsymbol{x}_{h_{t},i_{t}})\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" rspace="1.097em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R={\textstyle\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}}}R_{t},\hskip 9.24994ptR_{t}=\mathbb{E}\left[f_{1}^{*}(\boldsymbol{x}_{t}^{*})-f_{1}^{*}(\boldsymbol{x}_{h_{t},i_{t}})\right]</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}_{T}=[\boldsymbol{x}_{h,i}]_{(h,i)\in\mathcal{N}_{t},t\in\mathcal{T}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi></mrow></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒩</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">t</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒯</mi></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}_{T}=[\boldsymbol{x}_{h,i}]_{(h,i)\in\mathcal{N}_{t},t\in\mathcal{T}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> be the complete set of candidate merging weights selected up to time <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. By Lemma <a href="#ThmLemma1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ IV-D Theoretical Analysis ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we have <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{X}_{T}|\leq TN" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{X}_{T}|\leq TN</annotation></semantics></math>.
We first introduce the following assumption.</p>
</div>
<div id="ThmAssumption2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Assumption">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="ThmAssumption2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption 2</span></span><span id="ThmAssumption2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="ThmAssumption2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="ThmAssumption2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="ThmAssumption2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="ThmAssumption2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathcal{X}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathcal{X}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, it satisfies <math id="ThmAssumption2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\boldsymbol{x}\|_{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\boldsymbol{x}\|_{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="ThmAssumption2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\boldsymbol{x}]_{j}=[\boldsymbol{x}]_{j+V/2},\text{for }1\leq j\leq V/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">V</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic" mathsize="0.900em">for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">V</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\boldsymbol{x}]_{j}=[\boldsymbol{x}]_{j+V/2},\text{for }1\leq j\leq V/2</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore, for some <math id="ThmAssumption2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{0}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">&gt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{0}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the NTK matrix <math id="ThmAssumption2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{M}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="ThmAssumption2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{M}\succeq\lambda_{0}\boldsymbol{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐌</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">⪰</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐈</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{M}\succeq\lambda_{0}\boldsymbol{I}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This assumption is commonly used in neural bandit research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. The constraint <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\boldsymbol{x}\|_{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\boldsymbol{x}\|_{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math> is introduced to normalize the context vector. The symmetry condition enables the construction of an equivalent context vector in the form <math id="S4.SS4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}=[\boldsymbol{x}^{\top},\boldsymbol{x}^{\top}]^{\top}/\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝒙</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><msqrt><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{x}^{\prime}=[\boldsymbol{x}^{\top},\boldsymbol{x}^{\top}]^{\top}/\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="ThmTheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_Theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="ThmTheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="ThmTheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="ThmTheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="ThmTheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="ThmTheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="ThmTheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{d}</annotation></semantics></math> be the effective dimension of the NTK matrix <math id="ThmTheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{M}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="ThmTheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{m}=[f_{1}^{*}(\boldsymbol{x})]_{\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathcal{X}_{T}}^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐦</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">f</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒳</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{m}=[f_{1}^{*}(\boldsymbol{x})]_{\boldsymbol{x}\in\mathcal{X}_{T}}^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> as the true reward vector of the candidate merging weights.
There exist constants <math id="ThmTheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},C_{2}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">&gt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},C_{2}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="ThmTheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="ThmTheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w\geq\operatorname{poly}(T,L,K,\lambda^{-1},\lambda_{0}^{-1},S^{-1},\log(1/\delta))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">≥</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">poly</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">0</mn><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">S</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w\geq\operatorname{poly}(T,L,K,\lambda^{-1},\lambda_{0}^{-1},S^{-1},\log(1/\delta))</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="ThmTheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta\leq C_{1}(TdL+d\lambda)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">η</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta\leq C_{1}(TdL+d\lambda)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="ThmTheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\geq\max\{1,S^{-2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">≥</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">S</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\geq\max\{1,S^{-2}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="ThmTheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\geq\sqrt{2\boldsymbol{m}^{\top}\boldsymbol{M}^{-1}\boldsymbol{m}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">S</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">≥</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐦</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐌</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐦</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\geq\sqrt{2\boldsymbol{m}^{\top}\boldsymbol{M}^{-1}\boldsymbol{m}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then with probability at least <math id="ThmTheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, the regret of Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-C Design of Tanbr ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> satisfies,</span></p>
</div>
<div id="ThmTheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S4.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">≤</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R\leq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle 3\sqrt{T}\sqrt{\tilde{d}\log(1+TN/\lambda)+2}\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn><msqrt><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></msqrt><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em">⋅</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 3\sqrt{T}\sqrt{\tilde{d}\log(1+TN/\lambda)+2}\cdot</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left[\upsilon\sqrt{\tilde{d}\log(1+TN/\lambda)+2-2\log\delta}\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">υ</mi><msqrt><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left[\upsilon\sqrt{\tilde{d}\log(1+TN/\lambda)+2-2\log\delta}\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\left.+(\lambda+C_{3}TL)(1-\eta w\lambda)^{1/2}\sqrt{T/\lambda}+2\sqrt{\lambda S}\right]+T+1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn></msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">η</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">w</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow></msup><msqrt><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn><msqrt><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">S</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>]</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">+</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left.+(\lambda+C_{3}TL)(1-\eta w\lambda)^{1/2}\sqrt{T/\lambda}+2\sqrt{\lambda S}\right]+T+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark.</span> By treating <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> as constants and applying Lemma <a href="#ThmLemma1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ IV-D Theoretical Analysis ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the regret bound in Theorem <a href="#ThmTheorem1" title="Theorem 1. ‣ IV-D Theoretical Analysis ‣ IV Algorithm Design ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> simplifies to <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{T}=\mathcal{O}(\tilde{d}\sqrt{T}\log(T))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">R</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi></msqrt><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">T</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{T}=\mathcal{O}(\tilde{d}\sqrt{T}\log(T))</annotation></semantics></math>, matching the regret bounds in prior neural bandit studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>.
Compared to LinUCB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>, this bound includes an additional factor <math id="S4.SS4.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>d</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, reflecting the complexity of neural representations. This extra cost is acceptable given the ability to capture more expressive and nonlinear context.
A key contribution of <span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tanbr</span> is maintaining these theoretical guarantees in an uncountable and continuous decision space. This is achieved through smoothness assumptions and adaptive partitioning techniques that ensure efficient exploration while avoiding exhaustive search.
This result formally shows the theoretical suitability of <span id="S4.SS4.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tanbr</span> for long-term deployment in online MoE systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Performance Evaluation</span></h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">System Implementation</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">MoE Models and datasets<span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span>
We evaluate our <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tanbr</span> using both i) encoder-decoder, and ii) decoder-only Transformer models to ensure a comprehensive evaluation.
For both models, every FFN layer is replaced by an MoE layer containing <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">K</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=8</annotation></semantics></math> experts with the same architecture.
The encoder-decoder model is based on T5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, which includes <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> FFN layers in both the encoder and decoder, and has about <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1.0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0B</annotation></semantics></math> parameters. The decoder-only model is based on BERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and contains <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math> FFN layers with approximately <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="660M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">660</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">660M</annotation></semantics></math> parameters. Both models are pre-trained using task routing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Datasets<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span> We evaluate the models on <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">V</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V=8</annotation></semantics></math> common tasks from the GLUE benchmark, including natural language inference (<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, MNLI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite>, QNLI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, RTE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>), sentiment analysis (<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, SST-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>), sentence similarity (<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, MRPC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, QQP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>, STS-B <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite>), and linguistic acceptability (<span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i.e.</span>, CoLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite>).
Among them, CoLA is evaluated by the Matthews correlation coefficient, STS-B is evaluated by the average value of Pearson and Spearman correlation coefficients, and the remaining tasks are evaluated by accuracy. Each dataset is divided into training and validation sets in an <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="80/20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">80</mn><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">80/20</annotation></semantics></math> split.
Both the batch size and the maximum sequence length (for source and target) are set to <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="128" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">128</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">128</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Framework Setting<span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span> In <span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tanbr</span>, we set the smoothness parameter <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">ν</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{1}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0.3,0.5,0.7,0.9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.3</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.5</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.7</mn><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0.3,0.5,0.7,0.9\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The regularization parameter is <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math>, and the exploration parameter is <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\upsilon=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">υ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upsilon=1</annotation></semantics></math>. The confidence level is set as <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">δ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=1</annotation></semantics></math>. We use learning rate <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">η</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{1e-4,1e-3,1e-2,1e-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">4</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">3</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">e</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{1e-4,1e-3,1e-2,1e-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The neural network has width <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=64</annotation></semantics></math> and depth <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">L</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=2</annotation></semantics></math>. The maximum number of experts selected for merging is fixed to <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=8</annotation></semantics></math>. All experiments are run with PyTorch on NVIDIA A100 GPUs with <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40GB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">40</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40GB</annotation></semantics></math> of memory.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Baselines<span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span>
We compare <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tanbr</span> with the following methods:
i) <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RL</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite>: a reinforcement learning-based approach for expert selection in sparse MoE, assigning one expert per token.
ii) <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Switch</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>: a neural network-based method that learns to select one expert per token for sparse MoE routing.
iii) <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SMEAR</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>: a neural network-based token-level expert merging method.
iv) <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NUCB</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>: a neural bandit method that randomly samples a set of <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">20</span> feasible merging weights and selects one by neural UCB at each step.v) <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Regmean</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>: a model merging method that computes weights using a closed-form solution to the merging problem.
vi) <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Fisher</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>: a model merging method that leverages Fisher information to estimate parameter importance and performs weighted merging accordingly.
vii) <span id="S5.SS1.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Average</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>: a model merging method that performs element-wise averaging of all parameters.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Results with Fine-tuning and Inference</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Fine-tuning Phase<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">.</span></span> As mentioned in Section <a href="#S3" title="III System Model ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, T5-based MoE with post-layer normalization requires fine-tuning when expert merging is applied due to its sensitivity to parameter shifts. Therefore, we fine-tune its normalization layers for <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn mathsize="0.900em">10,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10,000</annotation></semantics></math> steps using the training data, while simultaneously training a new router with different algorithms.
The results are depicted in Fig. <a href="#S5.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ V-B Results with Fine-tuning and Inference ‣ V Performance Evaluation ‣ Faster, Smaller, and Smarter: Task-Aware Expert Merging for Online MoE Inference" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, showing the training loss curve, total training time, and memory usage.
From the figure, we have some observations.
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">First</span>, <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tanbr</span> starts with higher loss since it begins with fewer candidate merging weights. However, as the partition tree refines over time, <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tanbr</span> converges much faster and achieves lower final loss than all baselines. In comparison, <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Nucb</span>, which selects decisions from randomly generated merging weights, exhibits more fluctuation and slower convergence.
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Second</span>, both <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tanbr</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Nucb</span> (task-level routing) achieve the <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">smallest training time</span>, achieving approximately <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="40\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">40</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">40\%</annotation></semantics></math> reduction compared to <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RL</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Switch</span>. Although <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tanbr</span> introduces a small amount of overhead due to maintaining the partition tree, it remains highly efficient. In contrast, <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Smear</span>, which merges at the token level, results in a <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="20\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" mathsize="0.900em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\times</annotation></semantics></math> increase in computational cost because it cannot use <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lightweight adapters</span> under the given pre-trained MoE architecture, making its overhead unavoidable.
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Third</span>,
<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Tanbr</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.17" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Nucb</span> consume about <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> more memory than <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RL</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Switch</span>, as they need to maintain the loss gradients of multiple experts. <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Smear</span> performs token-level merging, incurring the highest memory footprint, exceeding other methods by <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="179\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn mathsize="0.900em">179</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">179\%</annotation></semantics></math> because it stores token-wise loss gradients for all experts.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="S5.F2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.F2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
